<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fourth Anglo-Dutch War was fought in 1780–1784 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade with the American rebels. The Dutch suffered heavy losses of shipping and colonial trade, a blow that exposed and accelerated the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war.jpg" alt="Fourth Anglo-Dutch War" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Luny · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_the_Dogger_Bank_5_August_1781.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> had been allies since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> had become the junior partner and had slowly lost their dominance of world trade. The republic stayed neutral in the early years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. Dutch merchants, especially those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, supplied arms and munitions to the American rebels, mainly through the Dutch island of St. Eustatius, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> entered the war in 1778 they also sold France naval stores.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> relied on the principle of free ship, free goods in the Anglo-Dutch Commercial Treaty of 1668. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared naval stores contraband and seized Dutch ships carrying them. After the affair of Fielding and Bylandt on 31 December 1779 and a Dutch request to join the First League of Armed Neutrality, Britain declared war in December 1780.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet had about 20 ships of the line and could not match the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. Within weeks more than 200 Dutch merchantmen, carrying cargo worth 15 million guilders, had been captured. In the West Indies Admiral Rodney took St. Eustatius on 3 February 1781, confiscated its goods and deported its merchants. In European waters the main fleet action was the Battle of Dogger Bank in August 1781, between Admiral Johan Zoutman and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyde-parker">Hyde Parker</a>, which ended in a tactical draw.</p><p>In Asian waters the British took Dutch Negapatnam in 1781 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a> in early 1782, though the French under the Bailli de Suffren recaptured Trincomalee in August 1782. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, Henry Botham of the British company at Fort Marlborough sailed for Padang with 100 company soldiers, and on 18 August 1781 Jacob van Heemskerk, the chief resident there, surrendered all the west coast outposts without a fight, yielding 500,000 florins in goods and money.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In April 1782 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a> obtained recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from the States General, which made the republic the second European power to recognise it. The republic joined the armistice between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in January 1783. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Negapatnam</a> a British colony, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and gave Britain the right of free trade with part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>.</p><p>The war proved a disaster for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, above all economically, and confirmed the weakening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> power in the 18th century. The stadtholder's opponents blamed his mismanagement and formed the Patriot party. A Prussian invasion of Holland suppressed the Patriot revolt in 1787, and in 1795 the Patriots returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>. The war also damaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, which was already in crisis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fourth Anglo-Dutch War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</b> (1780–1784) proved ruinous: the navy was outmatched, hundreds of Dutch merchant ships were captured, and the VOC's trade with Asia was crippled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</b> (1780–1784), provoked by Dutch trade with the American rebels, devastated shipping and exposed the navy’s weakness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
